   $q$A table shows what a dog walker earns. For $3$ hours the pay is 36 dollars, for $5$ hours it is 60 dollars, and for $8$ hours it is 96 dollars. Which equation gives the pay $y$, in dollars, for $x$ hours?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$x = 12y$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"swapped-variables"},{"text":"$y = 12x$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$y = x + 33$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"additive-rule-from-first-pair"},{"text":"$y = 36x$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-first-y-as-rate"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$y = 12x$$q$, $q$Each ratio of pay to hours is the same: $\frac{36}{3} = \frac{60}{5} = \frac{96}{8} = 12$, so $y = 12x$. The choice $y = x + 33$ fits only the first pair, since $5 + 33 = 38$, not $60$.$q$, 'draft', null)
